<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">School of the Americas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Western Hemisphere Institute for Security Cooperation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The School of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army training institution for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> military and police officers, founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a> in 1946. Moved to Fort Benning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1984 under the canal treaties, it was renamed the Western Hemisphere Institute for Security Cooperation in 2001. Critics have long condemned it because some graduates, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-noriega">Manuel Noriega</a>, were later involved in coups and human rights abuses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/school-of-the-americas.jpg" alt="School of the Americas" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WHINSEC-Seal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1946 the United States Army founded the Latin American Training Center-Ground Division at Fort Amador in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a>, to centralise the training of the growing number of Latin Americans attending American service schools there. It taught Latin American personnel to use artillery and advanced weapons bought from the United States and gave instruction in nation-building. Renamed the Latin American Ground School, it was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of engineering, communications, and weapons and tactics. Enrolment was low at first, as Latin American officials preferred training in the continental United States, and from 1947 officers and lower ranks were taught in separate classes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In February 1949 the army moved the school to Fort Gulick and named it the U.S. Army Caribbean School; that year it graduated 743 American and 251 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> students from ten countries. By 1954 its pupils were overwhelmingly Latin American, and in 1956 Spanish became its only language of instruction. In 1963 it was renamed the U.S. Army School of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. During the mid-1960s it was one of the places where the army trained soldiers in jungle warfare, among them men of the 1st Cavalry Division bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p>By the time Congress withdrew the Secretary of the Army's authority to run it in 2000, the school had graduated 60,428 officers, cadets, police and defence officials from 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Renamed the following year, it came under a Board of Visitors of 14 members, among them representatives of human rights, religious, academic and business communities. Venezuela stopped sending soldiers in 2004, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> on 28 March 2006, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> in 2008 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> in 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the early 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> critics accused the school of teaching techniques of repression. Since 1990 the organisation School of the Americas Watch has held a yearly protest at Fort Benning, which drew 19,000 people in 2005. It marks the killing on 16 November 1989 of six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests, their housekeeper Elba Ramos and her daughter Celia Marisela Ramos by the Atlácatl Battalion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> panel concluded that nineteen of the 27 killers were graduates. The school denied teaching torture, and Major Joseph Blair, a former instructor, said in 2002 that only the name had changed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Hemisphere Institute for Security Cooperation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Canal Zone held major American bases, including the headquarters of the US Southern Command and the <b>School of the Americas</b>, which trained Latin American officers there from 1946 to 1984.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Military</a></li></ul></section>
